; The revocation question, re-asked as a derivation (extension §2) for
; `modality-cross-check.sh`.
;
; `revocation-possible` is `live`, so this relation is the COUNTEREXAMPLE SET —
; the reachable situations from which "nobody is admin" can no longer be
; reached — and it is NON-EMPTY, because `writ check` reports this property as
; failing. That is the designed latch: grant-breakglass-admin has no inverse,
; so once it fires mallory is admin forever.
;
; Empty ⟺ the property holds. Note what that means for THIS file specifically:
; an inversion here — re-pointing `revocation-possible` at `can-still-revoke` —
; would NOT be caught by the cross-check, because both sets are non-empty in
; this scenario and both therefore read as `fails`. The `live` properties that
; catch an inversion are the ones that HOLD (oversight/accountability and both
; of workflow's), where the counterexample set is empty and the satisfying set
; is not. So the polarity of this file rests on the comment, not on the oracle.
; Do not "simplify" it into `can-still-revoke`.
;
; The `some` binder is the KERNEL's quantifier, evaluated by `Eval` inside the
; guard datum; `a` is lower-case on purpose, since an ALL-CAPS binder would read
; as a rule variable and be rejected (extension §4).

(load "ct.rules")

(satisfies no-admin (not (some (a account) (is a.role admin))))
(can-reach can-still-revoke no-admin)
(trapped revocation-possible can-still-revoke)
